+# Dispatcher paging identity correction — pending exact independent review
+
+This isolated continuation of 017f7ec9dae90361f09c6c5c8858b3534c95a281 addresses PG1 in the selected registered-agent inventory and its configured/card projection. Original 67d/017 source and proof remain immutable. The feature accepted root remains f122; this branch grants no full-parity, PR, runtime, permission or release acceptance.
+
+The exact admitted canonical Dispatcher directory is role-owned and excluded from ordinary Connect-to-Dispatcher inventory. A registered alias of that observed directory is also excluded, retaining both primary and selected-directory currentness evidence. A normal registered project's Connect-on primary that reuses the controller native ID remains visible with its own project/name and an explicit ambiguous endpoint association. It makes the page partial and contributes an unknown row; it never supplies configured directions/defaults. Native-ID equality alone does not prove canonical-role equality or global ID uniqueness. Ordinary restore/wiring behavior is unchanged.
+
+The existing 16-root/4096-visit/row and bounded primary-byte budgets, match/root continuation, selected role/project/source receipts and final authorization/runtime fences remain. Same-ID ambiguous entries occupy normal page slots and remain reachable across continuation. The canonical directory stat tuple is compared with the registered primary reader’s realpath/lstat root tuple verified through the chain around its held primary-file FD. Both selected-directory and primary receipts are retained through final assembly; retargeting an alias invalidates the evidence. Unsupported canonical directory identity makes the page unavailable; unsupported registered-root identity makes that root explicitly unavailable/partial. No path-string/name/native-ID equivalence substitutes for this observed tuple join. These are snapshots, not held root-directory handles, continuous locks, historical-incarnation proof or runtime authority; inode reuse/ABA limits remain.
+
+Controls use actual temporary registry/primary files and strict role/primary witnesses, actual inventory/configured readers and card projection. Endpoint assembly tests use the exact distinct controller/chat folders and real primary receipts, with modeled lifecycle/role-cache/actions-await ports. They test stable output, later ordinary-primary mutation and authorization priority. No live host/process/ordinary operational restore proof is claimed.
+
+Development history is preserved: original f9 test-only baseline on 017 had 3 failed tests, but its combined configured-page array assertion also expected one element in a legitimate eight-row page. A separate repaired test-only baseline separates page shape and first-entry identity. The first correction development run had 1 failed/75 passed, second had 4 failed/221 passed: the configured array expectation and new endpoint fixture's incorrect shared role folder caused those failures. Corrected focused development passes 225 tests across four files and tsc exits0; exact frozen proof is recorded separately rather than promoted from those development checks.
+
+Earlier proof assertion qualifications remain additive: parent-project RED fails the first hard result/count assertion before the separate PRIVATE_ assertion; byte-deferral RED fails its first boundary before later-page retrieval (the restored GREEN retrieves that page); cache-root RED passes withholding/no-name checks before unwanted extra callback/count failures. Those resource controls do not show disclosure.
+
+Cold tool/Canvas/zone/group/helper-parent sources and Quick paging remain on a separate unfinished branch. Full matrix, latest-main integration, independent final review and real-host acceptance remain open. Mini bp83o0 holds frozen custody only; source implementation ownership remains here until explicit verified takeover through parent/Navi/Tael. #3611 merge hold is superseded; #3706 public containing-desktop release hold remains. No Handoff APIs, job cancellations, releases or access changes.
